Version 0.1.0

o NEW: `control$max_condition` (default 1000) for `method = "hb"` in
  `add_population_error()` limits the condition number of the population
  correlation matrix with error. Estimating the true network (GLASSO) becomes
  very slow on nearly singular matrices (a single fit could take 20 seconds for
  54 nodes), which occur only at the largest errors the omitted edges can add.
  Across 46 networks, the limit removed 87% of the fitting time and kept nearly
  all of the reachable misfit (CFI, TLI, and SRMR peak well before the limit;
  RMSEA reach was 98% on average). A warning is given when `fit_value` needs
  more error than the limit allows; use `Inf` for no limit

o NEW: `seed` argument for reproducible results in `simulate_sbm()`,
  `simulate_smallworld()`, `add_population_error()`, `smallworldness()`,
  `proxswap_lattice()`, and `weibull_parameters()`; the same seed gives the same
  results and a seeded call does not change the R session's random number
  stream. All random numbers come from the package's C generators (random
  tie-breaking in edge weight assignment previously used R's random number
  generator)

o NEW: `network_fit()` returns class `"ggm_fit"` with `print()` and `summary()`
  methods (the named numeric vector is unchanged, e.g., `fit["CFI"]`);
  `print()`/`summary()` of simulated data report the realized and requested
  negative edges, generation diagnostics, and (for large networks) summaries
  instead of full matrices

o FIX: negative `skew` reversed the sign of a variable's correlations with all
  other variables (the transformation was negated); negative skew now reflects
  the data, skews, and reflects back. The skew transformation is fitted once per
  skew value on the standard normal distribution so that `skew` is the
  population skew (it was fitted to each sample's own skew, which could loop
  forever in small samples) and skewed variables are standardized with
  population moments. `skew` is rounded to 0.05 as documented, and `skew_range`
  draws one skew per variable (previously, a range with as many values as
  variables was applied in order)

o FIX: `simulate_sbm()` and `simulate_smallworld()` could loop forever when
  `snr` (e.g., above 2) predicts Weibull parameters outside the empirical
  bounds, and `simulate_smallworld()` when pruning could not reach a connected
  graph; Weibull parameters are now drawn from the bootstrapped parameters
  within the bounds (equivalent to the previous rejection sampling), an
  informative error is given when none exist, and pruning is limited to
  `max_iterations` attempts

o FIX: invalid `diffusion_range` or `skew_range` values gave internal errors (or
  were silently counted as rejected attempts); they are now checked with the
  other inputs. `blocks`, `nodes`, and `categories` must be whole numbers, and
  input checks no longer accept values slightly outside their range (values were
  rounded to three decimals) or missing values

o FIX: `add_population_error()` without `method` now gives the intended
  "`method` must be specified" error; `tolerance` must be positive

o CHANGE: after ridge conditioning, the Weibull parameters reported in
  `parameters$weibull` are re-estimated by maximum likelihood from the
  conditioned edge weights and re-checked against the empirical bounds (as
  documented); small-world rewired edges are assigned a distance of `neighbors +
  1` (as documented) and `simulate_smallworld()` returns named population
  matrices

o FIX: weighted `smallworldness()` treated larger weights as longer path lengths
  and ignored weights in the clustering coefficient; weighted path lengths now
  use inverse weights and clustering uses the weighted coefficient of Onnela et
  al. (2005), following Muldoon et al. (2016). SWP and SWI are bounded to [0, 1]
  as documented, self-loops are not counted as edges, simulated S uses the
  average random-graph clustering and path length, analytical S returns `NA`
  with a warning for an average degree of 1 or less, and multiple `method`
  values give an error

o FIX: `network_fit()` accepts data frames for `S`, checks its inputs, labels
  confidence intervals such as 97.5% correctly, and computes p-values without
  loss of precision

o FIX: `proxswap_lattice()` failed for a single pair of nodes at a ring distance
  and counted self-loops as edges; `weibull_parameters()` and `categorize()`
  check their inputs (`categorize()` returns integers, as documented);
  `simulate_smallworld()` no longer forces a negative edge for very small
  `negative_proportion`

o CHANGE: `add_population_error()` is faster: the target is found by searching
  upward from no error instead of a 20-point grid, evaluations are memoized,
  Cudeck-Browne error is scaled in closed form, decompositions are reused, and
  the reported fit comes from the optimization's refit of the true network
  (population values; equal to `network_fit()` of `Omega_pseudo` as the sample
  size approaches infinity); attempts continue until `fit_value` is matched (to
  1e-6) rather than stopping within `tolerance`

o FIX: the normal random number generator used the same low bits for the
  ziggurat strip and the value and was seeded with the clock only (parallel
  workers could share streams); it now uses independent bits and the same
  seeding as the uniform generator. Uniform draws are strictly between 0 and 1

o CHANGE: {psych} is no longer a dependency (skewness is computed internally)

o CHANGE: the default `fit_value` for `metric = "srmr"` in
  `add_population_error()` is 0.05 (previously 0.08), consistent with
  {latentFactoR}

o CHANGE: `population$P` from `add_population_error()` has been replaced by
  `population$Omega_error` (identical values: the saturated network with error)

o CHANGE: SRMR now follows {lavaan} (lower triangle including the diagonal) in
  `network_fit()` and `add_population_error()`

o FIX: `add_population_error()` treats non-converged constrained maximum
  likelihood refits as invalid, and the Wu and Browne (2015) method no longer
  rescales draws (draws with errors outside of -1 to 1 are infeasible)

o CHANGE: `weibull_parameters()` no longer uses sample size (`scaling`) as a
  predictor; the `sample_size` argument has been removed and both Weibull
  equations are now predicted from `snr` and `nodes` only. `sample_size` in
  `simulate_sbm()` and `simulate_smallworld()` no longer affects edge weights

o CHANGE: `simulate_sbm()` now balances the highest-weight (reserved) within-community
  edges across communities and guarantees a minimum of `nodes - 1`
  within-community edges per community, preventing nearly empty communities

o FIX: `simulate_sbm()` no longer fails with `diffusion = 1` (no reserved edges)

o NEW: initial release. Simulation tools moved from {L0ggm} (0.1.3):
  `simulate_sbm()`, `simulate_smallworld()`, `add_population_error()`,
  `smallworldness()`, `proxswap_lattice()`, `categorize()`, and
  `weibull_parameters()`

o NEW: `add_population_error()` gains `method` ("cb" Cudeck & Browne, 1992;
  "tkl" Tucker, Koopman, & Linn, 1969, where random minor factors with
  geometrically decaying loadings take a proportion of each node's residual
  variance, 1 - R^2, and the network is rescaled to the remaining variance so
  its partial correlations are unchanged; "wb" Wu & Browne, 2015; "hb" Hu & Bentler, 1998, where
  omitted edges, `control$omitted` (default 2), have random signs and partial
  correlations that vary uniformly between 0.5 and 1 times a common size that
  is optimized and are returned in `population_error$omitted`), `metric`
  ("CFI", "TLI", "RMSEA", "SRMR"), and `control` arguments. Fit indices are
  population values (no sample size): RMSEA = sqrt(F / df),
  CFI = 1 - F / F0, and TLI = 1 - (F / df) / (F0 / df0), where F is the maximum
  likelihood discrepancy, F0 = -log|Sigma| is the discrepancy of the
  independence model, and df0 = p(p - 1)/2. `fit_value` is now a numeric
  target value (defaults to the conventional cutoff of `metric`). `method` has
  no default and must be specified. `metric` defaults to the fit index
  consistent with `method` ("CFI" for "cb" and "tkl", "RMSEA" for "wb", and
  "SRMR" for "hb"). Errors are always limited to the range -1 to 1 and the
  population correlation matrix with error is always positive definite
